The constructor is a special method.

It runs automatically when you create an object using new.	 
Lifecycle methods are methods provided by React mainly for class components. 
They allow us to run code at different stages of a component's life

                    Component created
						  ↓
					Component displayed
						  ↓
					Component updated
						  ↓
					Component removed
					
					
1. Mounting — component is created	
Mounting means the component is created and added to the UI/DOM.
The important lifecycle methods are:
constructor()					
class User extends React.Component {
    constructor(props) {
        super(props);

        this.state = {
            name: "Renuka"
        };
    }
}

2. componentDidMount()
Runs after the component is added to the DOM
usually for Api calls

componentDidMount() {
    fetch("/api/users")
        .then(response => response.json())
        .then(data => {
            this.setState({ users: data });
        });
}

2. Updating — component changes

A component updates when its state or props change.

componentDidUpdate()

It runs after the component has been updated.

componentDidUpdate(prevProps, prevState) {

    if (prevState.name !== this.state.name) {
        console.log("Name changed");
    }
}

3> Unmounting — component is removed
componentWillUnmount()

Runs just before the component is removed from the DOM.

It is commonly used for cleanup.

For example, if you started a timer:
componentDidMount() {
    this.timer = setInterval(() => {
        console.log("Running");
    }, 1000);
}
You should clean it up:

componentWillUnmount() {
    clearInterval(this.timer);
}

| Phase      | Method                   | Purpose                          |
| ---------- | ------------------------ | -------------------------------- |
| Mounting   | `constructor()`          | Initialize state                 |
| Mounting   | `componentDidMount()`    | After component loads; API calls |
| Updating   | `componentDidUpdate()`   | After state/props update         |
| Unmounting | `componentWillUnmount()` | Cleanup                          |


Lifecycle methods are methods available mainly in React
class components that allow us to execute code during different stages of a component's lifecycle.
The main stages are mounting, updating, and unmounting.
For example, componentDidMount is used after the component is mounted,
componentDidUpdate runs after updates, and componentWillUnmount is used for cleanup before the component is removed

